I completely understand the goal to protect the container team's bandwidth for Q3/Q4. If this were purely a caching layer, scaling the existing external cluster would be fine. However, because we are relying on this for session persistence, the latency penalty of constantly traversing the OpenShift egress controllers and corporate firewalls for every Spring Boot microservice request will severely impact the application's response time for the associates.

By using the Redis Enterprise Operator inside OCP, we eliminate those network hops entirely. Furthermore, the Operator automates the lifecycle management, meaning we aren't asking the container team to manually manage stateful Kubernetes workloads. It acts as an automated DBA, which should actually reduce cross-team troubleshooting overhead in the long run.
